PRE-TABLE BLOCK -- #495 wdcaddtoinbufbound
Written 2026-08-30 BEFORE the product edit and BEFORE table 1, so this file's own
mtime proves the order.  Recorded OUTPUT, not assertion.

SCOPE: this round SHIPS a kill-table-backed detector, so the block is MANDATORY.

*** IT OPENS WITH A FLOOR FINDING THAT IS ALREADY MEASURED, BECAUSE IT DECIDES WHAT
THE ROUND MAY SHIP. ***

    base (defect LIVE)   128 rows,  0 failures
    fixA (write-arm)     128 rows,  0 failures
    fixB (re-arm)        128 rows,  1 failure
    mutA1               128 rows, 14 failures
    mutA2, mutA3        128 rows,  0 failures

**THE SHIPPED 128-ROW DETECTOR DOES NOT OBSERVE THIS DEFECT AT ALL** -- `base` is clean
with the defect live.  Vacuity-floor clause (a) is therefore NOT satisfiable from any
existing row, so **the rung-3 witness must ship AS the detector** or the round ships
below the floor.  That is not a preference; it is the only way clause (a) can be met.

====================================================================================
1.  SIBLING PARITY
====================================================================================

The diff guards the data-port WRITE arm.  Its nearest sibling is the data-port READ
arm in the same `wd_data` case.

ASYMMETRY FOUND, and it is deliberate rather than an omission:

  WRITE arm (guarded)   pushes into the ring; observable through ring occupancy, the
                        bytes the guest reads back, and the re-arm's contents
  READ  arm (untouched) pops from the ring; observable through the same channels

  >  The fix touches ONE side of a FIFO.  A reader might expect the read arm to need
  >  a matching guard -- it does not, and the reason is worth recording: a read
  >  cannot INTRODUCE foreign bytes.  It can only return what is there.  The defect
  >  is injection, and injection has exactly one door.

  >  BUT THE SIBLING RELATIONSHIP IS LOAD-BEARING IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, and this is
  >  the round's non-obvious dependency: fixA is safe ONLY because `#477`'s READ-arm
  >  phase test stops a stray read from moving `atapi_phase` off CMDOUT mid-CDB.
  >  Remove that and this guard starts refusing legitimate CDB bytes.  **fixA's
  >  correctness is not self-contained.**  Measured and named by the pass-1 measuring
  >  seat; recorded in the shipped comment, not only here.

ROW OWED: an accept row proving CDB collection still lands -- the `pkt_cdb_case` row
already in the shipped detector covers it (dispatch == 1 from six CMDOUT writes) and
reds if the guard over-refuses into CMDOUT.  Verified present; no new row owed.

====================================================================================
2.  CLAIM -> ROW  (semantic trigger: does the sentence assert something a row could
    observe?  Counterfactual "would <consequence>" clauses qualify.)
====================================================================================

  C1  "a packet command is active but we are not collecting a CDB, so these bytes
       belong to no transfer"
        -> ROW: the witness's G/H/I rows -- first three bytes after the re-arm.
           MEASURED: base a5/5a/c2 (injected, injected, shifted) vs fix c2/c3/c4 (disk)
  C2  "refuse the PUSH ONLY -- the access is still handled and falls through to the
       common tail"
        -> ROW: *** OWED AND NOT YET BUILT.  No row asserts the RETURN VALUE. ***  The
           carrier's own sketch rule says a handler's return value is a contract and
           that nobody checked it in the round where that rule was minted.  A `0` here
           would raise EXCEPTION_DBE and spin the guest silently -- measured last round
           at 19,793 diagnostics and ZERO `non-existant`.  **The round owes either a row
           or an explicit no-row sentence with its reason.**
  C3  "fixA is safe only because #477's read-arm phase test holds"
        -> NO ROW, reason recorded: the dependency is on ANOTHER round's guard, and a
           row that deletes #477's test to observe this one is a mutant of #477, not of
           this fix.  It belongs in #477's contract, not here.  **Stated rather than
           left blank** -- an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the
           same.

====================================================================================
3.  GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
====================================================================================

PREDICATE reads:   d->atapi_cmd_in_progress     (set at packet dispatch, cleared only
                                                 by wdc_new_command_reset())
                   d->atapi_phase               (PHASE_CMDOUT / PHASE_DATAIN / ...)

GUARDED BODY reads/writes:
                   idata, len                   (the access itself)
                   wdc_addtoinbuf() -> d->inbuf, d->inbuf_head, d->inbuf_tail

WHERE THEY DIFFER -- and this is the disagreeing state the check exists to find:

  >  `atapi_cmd_in_progress` and `atapi_phase` are written at DIFFERENT SITES.  The
  >  flag is set once at dispatch and cleared only by the reset helper; the phase is
  >  assigned at several points during a transfer.  **So a state exists where the flag
  >  is set and the phase is stale** -- which is exactly the family `#479` closed
  >  (`wdcwipclear`, `wdcringnoflush`) and `#477` guarded against on the read arm.
  >
  >  ROW OWED: a stale-phase state.  The shipped detector already carries
  >  `row_stale_phase_write` and the three `midxfer_case` rows, whose fixtures were
  >  re-pinned specifically so a phase-keying fix does not false-fail.  Verified
  >  present.  **No new row owed, and that is a finding rather than an absence: the
  >  disagreeing state is already covered because an earlier round paid for it.**

